class StyleObject(object):
    def __init__(self, name, rules, priority, inline=True):
        self.name = name
        self.rules = rules
        self.priority = priority
        self.inline = inline

    def to_css(self):
        if self.inline:
            return f'{self.name} {{\n {"""".join([f""  {k}: {v}

column3:
\n"" for k, v in self.rules.items()])}}}'

    def __lt__(self, other):
        if self.priority != other.priority:
            return self.priority < other.priority
        else: 
            return self.name < other.name

class StyleManager(object):
    def __init__(self):
        self._styles = []

    def add_style(self, style):
        self._styles.append(style)

    def remove_style(self, name):
        for i in range(len(self._styles)):
            if self._styles[i].name == name:
                del self._styles[i]
                return 

    def update_rule(self, name, rule, value):
        for style in self._styles:
            if style.name == name:
                style.rules[rule] = value

    def to_css(self):
        self._styles.sort() 
        return """".join([style.to_css() for style in self._styles])
```

In the above code, `StyleObject` represents a single style object that has properties such as name (a string), rules (a dictionary of CSS properties and their values), priority (an integer), and whether it is inline or not. The method `to_css` generates the corresponding CSS content for this style object.

The class `StyleManager` manages a list of styles, providing methods to add, remove, and update styles based on their names. It also provides a method `to_css` that generates the final CSS content from all the styles in the correct priority order. 

Remember that this is only an example and does not include some requested features such as support for custom properties within each CSS rule, media queries, responsive design, inheritance, etc. Implementing these features will require a more complex solution and likely involves using regular expressions to parse and manipulate the CSS strings directly.
